    "indicator_code": "30771885",
    "describe": "The federal funds target range is the band for the overnight lending rate set by the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) of the Federal Reserve through its monetary-policy decisions, representing the Fed's core tool for steering monetary policy. The data are a not-seasonally-adjusted absolute level that directly reflects the policy-rate setting after each meeting, usually expressed as an upper and lower bound (for example, 4.25 to 4.50 percent), with no year-on-year or month-on-month conversion required. The federal funds rate is one of the most closely watched policy rates in global financial markets; its movements directly affect bank lending costs, bond yields, equity valuations, and exchange rates, with far-reaching transmission to consumption, investment, and inflation, making it a core reference for investors gauging the Fed's policy stance and market liquidity conditions.",
